  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$36.19B** of miscellaneous chemical products in 2025, down 2.8% on 2024 and 12% below the 2022 peak ($41.19B). Diagnostic and laboratory reagents (3822) are the largest group at **31%** ($11.20B, up 4.4%), followed by other chemical preparations (3824, $5.64B), catalysts (3815, $4.51B), pesticides (3808, $4.49B) and fuel and lubricant additives (3811, $2.97B). Canada ($5.59B) and Mexico ($4.02B) buy 27% between them; the EU as a whole takes $8.32B.\n\nTariffs are zero for most of this trade: Canada, Mexico, South Korea and Singapore charge nothing on qualifying U.S. goods, and the EU cut its duties to zero on July 1, 2026 (except 3809.10 and 3824.60). **China** is the exception: it adds a 10% extra duty to MFN rates of up to 13%, and U.S. exports there fell 21.3%. What usually decides access is product approval — pesticide registration (PMRA in Canada, MAPA in Brazil, the EU rules), diagnostic-device approval for reagents, and chemical registration such as REACH and K-REACH — plus U.S. rules such as FIFRA export labeling, TSCA 12(b) export notices and RIN retirement for biodiesel.",
  "latestYear": 2025,
  "priorYear": 2024,
